# Suspicious Encoded Command or Scripting Interpreter Execution

## Overview

Command and scripting interpreters are a built-in feature of nearly every operating system. Windows ships with both `cmd.exe` and PowerShell (`powershell.exe`, `pwsh.exe`), macOS and Linux ship with a POSIX shell — and all of them give an operator, or an adversary, a fast and flexible way to interact directly with the underlying system.

Because these interpreters are trusted, pre-installed, and rarely restricted, adversaries frequently abuse them post-exploitation to run commands, drop payloads, or move laterally without introducing new binaries that might trip signature-based defenses. On Windows specifically, PowerShell's `-EncodedCommand` (and its short-form aliases `-e`, `-en`, `-ec`, `-enc`) accepts a Base64-encoded UTF-16LE string as an entire script block. This is a legitimate feature — it exists to safely pass complex scripts through the command line without quoting issues — but it is also one of the most common ways adversaries obscure malicious PowerShell from static string-matching, casual log review, and some legacy AV signatures.

This detection targets that specific behavior: PowerShell or Command Prompt invocations whose command line references an encoded-command switch or contains the literal string `base64`, regardless of case.

## Why This Matters

Encoding a command doesn't make it more capable — it makes it harder to *read*. That's the entire point from an attacker's perspective:

- It defeats simple keyword/string-based detections looking for plaintext IOCs (`Invoke-Mimikatz`, `IEX (New-Object Net.WebClient)`, etc.), since the malicious string only exists in decoded form at runtime.
- It complicates manual triage — an analyst has to decode Base64 before they can even tell what the command does.
- It's cheap and trivial to generate, so it shows up across a wide range of TTPs: initial payload staging, in-memory execution, defense evasion, and living-off-the-land (LOLBin) techniques.

Because of this, "encoded command" itself is a weak standalone IOC — it's common in legitimate enterprise tooling too (RMM agents, EDR remediation scripts, config management, vulnerability scanners). The value of this rule comes less from the raw match and more from tuning: pairing it with your environment's known-good encoders, and routing what survives the filters into a proper investigation rather than an auto-close.

